INT. HILLTOP OBSERVATORY - DOME - NIGHT

A great white dome, its slit open to a scatter of stars. The telescope rises through the center like the barrel of a cathedral organ. A spiral stair, a brass gantry, a desk strewn with charts. A radiator ticks. Wind sighs through the slit.

NOELLE FAIRCHILD-ABARA, 30s, in a thick sweater and fingerless gloves, leans against the telescope pier, arms wrapped around herself. Her wedding ring is gone. A pale band marks the finger where it used to be. On the gantry above her, WILFRED OYELOLA-GRAY, 30s, a postdoc, adjusts a mirror, whistling quietly. Behind them both, at the foot of the stair, sits PROFESSOR IOLANTHE VASQUEZ-KIM, 70s, in a wheelchair with a shawl over her knees, a thermos in her lap, grading papers by flashlight.

NOELLE
(to the dark)
He's late.

WILFRED
He's always late.

NOELLE
He said eight.

WILFRED
He said eight o'clock, one of those eights.

IOLANTHE
(not looking up)
Eight o'clock the way he says it is a state of mind.

Noelle laughs, once, then bites it off. She pulls her sleeves down over her hands. A phone buzzes in her pocket. She pulls it out, reads the screen, and her face hardens. She walks to the desk, props the phone against a stack of star charts, and presses the speaker.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
Noelle? I'm so sorry, I got held up.

NOELLE
Where are you?

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
The airport. The flight's delayed.

NOELLE
Which airport?

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
What do you mean?

NOELLE
Marcus, there are three. Which one?

A pause on the line. Wilfred stops whistling. Iolanthe lowers her flashlight. Marcus draws a breath and exhales it.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm not at the airport.

NOELLE
No.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm at the house.

NOELLE
Which house?

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
Hers.

The silence in the dome is enormous. Wilfred climbs down from the gantry rung by rung. Noelle does not move. She stares at the phone as if she expected it to say something else. The radiator ticks. The wind sighs through the slit in the roof, and a single star drifts into the frame of the telescope eyepiece unobserved.

NOELLE
You asked me to come here tonight.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I know.

NOELLE
You said you wanted to show me something.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I did. I do. I was going to.

NOELLE
You were going to propose a toast to the comet.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
Noelle...

NOELLE
No. Say it plainly.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I can't do it anymore. I'm sorry. I should have told you before you drove all the way up.

NOELLE
Twelve years.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I know.

NOELLE
And you couldn't tell me to my face.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I thought it would be kinder.

NOELLE
It would have been kinder to tell me in the car, in the kitchen, in the dark. Anywhere but a place where we once watched the sky open up.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over phone)
I know. I'm sorry.

She says nothing. She stares at the phone. After a moment she reaches out and presses the button that ends the call. The screen goes dark. The dome is very still.

Wilfred picks up a blanket from the gantry rail and drapes it over her shoulders. Iolanthe wheels herself forward, one slow push at a time, until her chair bumps the leg of the desk. She unscrews the thermos and pours something steaming into the cap.

IOLANTHE
Drink.

NOELLE
What is it?

IOLANTHE
Coffee and something worse.

Noelle takes the cap and drinks. She coughs. Iolanthe nods, satisfied.

IOLANTHE (CONT'D)
My husband left me for a bassoonist in 1979.

NOELLE
What did you do?

IOLANTHE
I discovered two asteroids and named one after his mother.

WILFRED
(softly)
Professor.

IOLANTHE
It's a very ugly asteroid.

Noelle laughs, helplessly, with tears in her eyes. Wilfred steps to the console and flips a switch. The great dome begins to turn. The slit sweeps past the horizon and across the Milky Way, and the telescope swings after it with a deep, patient rumble.

WILFRED
Come see.

NOELLE
See what?

WILFRED
Something that isn't going anywhere.

She wipes her face. She climbs the stair, one step at a time, the blanket trailing behind her, and puts her eye to the lens. A long breath. The stars fill her sight.
